@prefix propp: <https://bookwright.dev/vocab/propp#> .
@prefix crm:   <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

# Propp's 31 narrative functions as a controlled vocabulary (iteration 030). Each
# term is a `crm:E55_Type` individual carrying an English and a Spanish
# `rdfs:label`; a narrative function (G10) whose authored name matches one (by
# `make_slug`, ES- or EN-spelled) is typed against it via `crm:P2_has_type`.
#
# These terms are Bookwright's, NOT part of the frozen GOLEM ontology
# (`golem.ttl`): they are intentionally outside the `CLASS_IRI` 17-class closure
# (Constitution X). The emitted `bible/graph.ttl` reuses only `crm:E55_Type` for
# typing; no new GOLEM/ontology class is introduced (FR-002). The loader
# (`io/vocabularies.py`) reads the labels here as the single source of truth —
# Python hardcodes no term URI (research D2). Subjects use full IRIs (not the
# `propp:` prefix) because a `/` is illegal in a Turtle prefixed name — mirroring
# how `sources.ttl` writes its E55_Type individuals.
#
# Function #8 is Propp's combined villainy/lack slot (A/a): it carries all four
# names as labels, so any of villainy / lack / fechoría / carencia types to it.

<https://bookwright.dev/vocab/propp#function/absentation>
    a crm:E55_Type ; rdfs:label "absentation"@en, "alejamiento"@es .
<https://bookwright.dev/vocab/propp#function/interdiction>
    a crm:E55_Type ; rdfs:label "interdiction"@en, "prohibición"@es .
<https://bookwright.dev/vocab/propp#function/violation>
    a crm:E55_Type ; rdfs:label "violation"@en, "transgresión"@es .
<https://bookwright.dev/vocab/propp#function/reconnaissance>
